<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Cumae</h1><p class="st-meta">740 BCE – 421 BCE (approx.) · capital Cumae</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The oldest Greek colony on the Italian mainland, home of the Sibyl, which passed the Greek alphabet to the Etruscans and Latins.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Euboean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eretria">Eretria</a> founded Cumae around 740 BCE on the Campanian coast north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a>, having first settled the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pithekoussai">Pithekoussai</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a>). The site, a steep acropolis above the sea, controlled the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campi-flegrei">Phlegraean Fields</a> and gave access to Etruscan and native trade.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cumae was an aristocratic city that became a major power in the Tyrrhenian world. Its version of the Greek alphabet was adopted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and, through them, by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The cave of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumaean-sibyl">Sibyl</a> and the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> made it a famous oracle. The tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristodemus-of-cumae">Aristodemus</a> defeated the Etruscans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aricia">Aricia</a> around 504 BCE, and in 474 BCE Cumae and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiero-i-of-syracuse">Hieron I of Syracuse</a> destroyed an Etruscan fleet off the city. Cumae also founded <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/neapolis">Neapolis</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 421 BCE Samnite-speaking Campanians captured Cumae, and it became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> town. It received Roman citizenship without the vote in 338 BCE, later became a Roman municipium and declined as nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pozzuoli">Puteoli</a> grew. A Byzantine fortress in the 6th century CE, it was finally destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in 1207.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cumae.jpg" alt="Cumae" width="320" height="180"><figcaption>Image: Saverio.G · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Parco_archeologico_Cuma_15.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>740 BCE – 421 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cumae</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aristodemus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kyme, Cuma</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-580&amp;focus=cumae" aria-label="Cumae on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5024 719 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cumae lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5344 975L5347 967M5344 975L5383 1098"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5347 967h0M5383 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5344 975h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5344 975h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-580&amp;focus=cumae">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

